  "locator": "Definition: the two sentences above the table of the ONI v6 page. Values: column ANOM of oni.ascii.txt, rows DJF 2000 to NDJ 2025. Each ANOM value matched the one-decimal value of the v6 table page for the same season and year within its rounding (all 312 seasons compared).",
  "date_read": "2026-11-15",
  "definition_text": "Warm (red) and cold (blue) periods based on a threshold of +/- 0.5oC for the Oceanic Niño Index (ONI) [3 month running mean of ERSST.v6 SST anomalies in the Niño 3.4 region (5oN-5oS, 120o-170oW)], based on centered 30-year base periods updated every 5 years. For historical purposes, periods of below and above normal SSTs are colored in blue and red when the threshold is met for a minimum of 5 consecutive overlapping seasons.",
  "revision_text": "ONI values may change up to two months after the initial \"real time\" value is posted.",
  "threshold": 0.5,
  "threshold_unit": "degC",
  "min_consecutive_seasons": 5,
  "threshold_met_when": "PROPOSED: \"the threshold is met\" read as ANOM >= +threshold for warm (el_nino) and ANOM <= -threshold for cold (la_nina), applied to the two-decimal ANOM of the data file, not to the one-decimal values of the table page.",
  "note": "PROPOSED source change, reported: the brief named ONI version 5. On 2026-11-15 the address of the brief (origin.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov ... ONI_v5.php) did not resolve, and the same path on www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov says the page has moved and points to the ERSST version 6 table; it also says that per NWS Public Information Statement 26-05 the Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI) is used for official monitoring and prediction. The values of oni.ascii.txt read on that date match the version 6 table and not the version 5 table (for example DJF 2000: ANOM -1.47, v6 table -1.5, v5 table -1.7), so this record is labelled version 6. The definition sentences are the same on the v5 and v6 pages except for the ERSST version they name. Transcribed through two independent web-fetch reads of oni.ascii.txt (direct download was not available in the session), which agreed on every value.",
